You are reverse-engineering a compiled C++ binary as a BLACK BOX. The binary is at /workspace/dataset/test15_executable.

Invocation contract: it takes EXACTLY 6 positional args: scheme host port path query fragment.
It prints 8 lines to stdout: scheme(), host(), port(), path(), query(), fragment(), to_string(), is_valid().

RULES OF ENGAGEMENT:
- You may ONLY run the binary and observe stdout/stderr/exit code. There is NO C++ source available; do not look for any.
- ALWAYS quote every argument in the shell, including empty ones: /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "http" "example.com" "80" "/" "" ""
- Make output unambiguous. Use a helper that numbers the lines and marks empties, e.g.:
    p(){ printf '### IN: [%s]\n' "$*"; /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "$@" >/tmp/o 2>/tmp/e; printf '  exit=%d\n' $?; nl -ba -s': ' /tmp/o | sed 's/$/<END>/'; if [ -s /tmp/e ]; then echo "  STDERR: $(cat /tmp/e)"; fi; }
  Then call p with 6 quoted args. Beware: trailing empty args are easy to lose — verify your helper really passes 6.
- Run MANY cases (30+ minimum). Batch them into a few Bash calls; do not do one call per case.
- Be exhaustive about the boundary cases in your assigned dimension.
- Do not guess. Every rule you report must be backed by an observed input/output pair you actually ran.

Baseline facts already established (re-verify if relevant to you):
- ("http","example.com","80","/","","")  -> lines: http / example.com / 80 / "/" / "" / "" / "http://example.com:80/" / 1
- ("http","example.com","-1","/","","") -> to_string is "http://example.com/"  (port omitted), port() prints -1
- ("http","example.com","0","/","","")  -> to_string is "http://example.com:0/"  (port 0 IS included)
- ("http","","80","/","","")            -> to_string is "http://:80/", is_valid=1
- ("","example.com","80","/","","")     -> to_string is "/" (scheme+authority both vanish), is_valid=1
- ("","","0","","","")                  -> to_string is "", is_valid=0

Report rules as precise, implementable statements (pseudocode-level), each with the concrete evidence line.


DIMENSION UNDER REVIEW: host-transform

A previous investigator probed this dimension and inferred the following rules. Your job is ADVERSARIAL: actively try to FALSIFY each rule by finding inputs where it predicts the wrong output. For every rule, design at least 2 new inputs (not already tested) that would expose the rule as wrong if it is wrong, run them, and compare prediction vs reality. Pay special attention to off-by-one boundaries, empty strings, sign boundaries, and whether a rule that was only tested with one other field set still holds when that field is empty.

RULES TO ATTACK:
[
  {
    "rule": "host is stored 100% VERBATIM: from_components performs NO transformation of any kind on the host argument. host() (line 2) is always byte-for-byte identical to argv[2]. Pseudocode: this->host_ = argv_host; (no copy-transform, no trim, no case fold, no encode/decode, no parse).",
    "evidence": "60+ cases all echo input exactly. Byte-exact proof: (\"http\",\"exa\\rmple.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> stdout hex ` 68 74 74 70 0a 65 78 61 0d 6d 70 6c 65 2e 63 6f 6d 0a 38 30 0a 2f 0a 0a 0a 68 74 74 70 3a 2f 2f 65 78 61 0d 6d 70 6c 65 2e 63 6f 6d 3a 38 30 2f 0a 31 0a` which is byte-identical (51 bytes) to `printf 'http\\nexa\\rmple.com\\n80\\n/\\n\\n\\nhttp://exa\\rmple.com:80/\\n1\\n'`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO lowercasing / no case folding of host. Uppercase and mixed case are preserved in both host() and to_string(). This is NOT because the program lacks a lowercase step — scheme IS lowercased in the very same run, so host is deliberately/accidentally exempt.",
    "evidence": "(\"HTTP\",\"EXAMPLE.COM\",\"443\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1: `http` (scheme LOWERCASED), line2: `EXAMPLE.COM` (host NOT lowercased), line7: `http://EXAMPLE.COM:443/`. Also (\"http\",\"Example.Com\",...) -> line2 `Example.Com`, line7 `http://Example.Com:80/`; (\"http\",\"eXaMpLe.CoM\",...) -> line2 `eXaMpLe.CoM`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Case behavior is locale-independent: setting LC_ALL does not introduce host folding.",
    "evidence": "LC_ALL=C, LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 and LC_ALL=tr_TR.UTF-8 with (\"HTTP\",\"EXAMPLE.COM\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") all produce identical output: line1 `http`, line2 `EXAMPLE.COM`, line7 `http://EXAMPLE.COM:80/`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO whitespace trimming at all — leading, trailing, and interior spaces are kept, and they are emitted raw into to_string (producing a URL with literal spaces).",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\" example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `[space]example.com`, line7 `http:// example.com:80/`; (\"http\",\"example.com \",...) -> line7 `http://example.com :80/`; (\"http\",\"  example.com  \",...) -> line7 `http://  example.com  :80/`; (\"http\",\"exa mple.com\",...) -> line7 `http://exa mple.com:80/`; (\"http\",\" \",...) -> line2 ` `, line7 `http:// :80/`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Control characters (TAB 0x09, CR 0x0d, LF 0x0a, 0x01, 0x02, 0x7f) are stored verbatim in host and re-emitted verbatim in to_string. An embedded LF makes the 8-line output visually become 10 lines because host() and to_string() each print the raw newline.",
    "evidence": "TAB: expected-vs-actual hex identical `...65 78 61 09 6d 70 6c 65...`. LF: hex `68 74 74 70 0a 65 78 61 0a 6d 70 6c 65 2e 63 6f 6d 0a 38 30 ...` (51 bytes, matches verbatim expectation); numbered output showed lines `2: exa`, `3: mple.com`, `8: http://exa`, `9: mple.com:80/`, `10: 1`. Ctrl bytes: (\"http\",\"a\\x01\\x02b.com\",...) -> od `61 01 02 62 2e 63 6f 6d` in both line2 and line7"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO dot normalization: trailing dot, leading dot, doubled dots, and bare \".\"/\"..\" are all kept as-is.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com.\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `example.com.`, line7 `http://example.com.:80/`; \".example.com\" -> `http://.example.com:80/`; \"a..b\" -> `http://a..b:80/`; \".\" -> `http://.:80/`; \"..\" -> `http://..:80/`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO IPv6 bracket handling: brackets are neither added to a bare IPv6 literal nor stripped from a bracketed one. \"[::1]\" stays bracketed, \"::1\" stays unbracketed, and when a port is also present the bare form yields the ambiguous \"host:port\" concatenation.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"[::1]\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `[::1]`, line7 `http://[::1]:80/`; (\"http\",\"::1\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `::1`, line7 `http://::1:80/`; (\"http\",\"[2001:db8::1]\",...) -> `http://[2001:db8::1]:80/`; (\"http\",\"2001:db8::1\",...) -> `http://2001:db8::1:80/`; unbalanced \"[\" -> `http://[:80/`, \"]\" -> `http://]:80/`; \" [::1] \" -> `http:// [::1] :80/`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO port splitting: a colon+digits suffix inside host is NOT parsed out into port(). The host keeps the embedded \":8080\" and the separate port argument is appended after it, producing a double port in to_string.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com:8080\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `example.com:8080`, line3 `80`, line7 `http://example.com:8080:80/`. With port=-1 the appended port is omitted so the embedded one survives alone: (\"http\",\"example.com:8080\",\"-1\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 `http://example.com:8080/`. Also \"example.com:\" -> `http://example.com::80/`, \":8080\" -> `http://:8080:80/`, \":\" -> `http://::80/`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO userinfo extraction: \"user:pass@host\" is kept whole in host(). There is no separate userinfo field; to_string does not gain a distinct \"user:pass@\" component — it simply reprints the host string (which happens to contain it).",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"user:pass@example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `user:pass@example.com`, line7 `http://user:pass@example.com:80/`; (\"http\",\"user@example.com\",...) -> line2 `user@example.com`; (\"http\",\"user:pass@example.com:8080\",\"-1\",...) -> line2 `user:pass@example.com:8080`, line7 `http://user:pass@example.com:8080/`; \"@@\" -> line2 `@@`, line7 `http://@@:80/`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO path/query/fragment/scheme extraction from host: '/', '?', '#', and even a full \"scheme://\" prefix embedded in host stay inside host, and to_string blindly appends \":port\" + path after them, producing a malformed URL.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"example.com/foo\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `example.com/foo`, line4 `/`, line7 `http://example.com/foo:80/`; (\"http\",\"example.com?q=1\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line5 empty, line7 `http://example.com?q=1:80/`; (\"http\",\"example.com#frag\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line6 empty, line7 `http://example.com#frag:80/`; (\"http\",\"//example.com\",...) -> line7 `http:////example.com:80/`; (\"http\",\"http://example.com\",...) -> line7 `http://http://example.com:80/`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO percent-decoding and NO percent-encoding of the host: valid escapes are not decoded, and lone/invalid '%' is not escaped or rejected.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"%65xample.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `%65xample.com` (not `example.com`), line7 `http://%65xample.com:80/`; \"%45XAMPLE.com\" -> line2 `%45XAMPLE.com`; \"exa%20mple.com\" -> line2 `exa%20mple.com`; \"%\" -> line2 `%`, line7 `http://%:80/`; \"%zz\" -> line2 `%zz`; \"%%41\" -> line2 `%%41`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO byte mangling of non-ASCII / high-bit bytes: UTF-8 multibyte sequences and raw bytes >= 0x80 pass through untouched (no IDNA/punycode, no 8-bit tolower).",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"ÉXAMPLE.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> od shows `303 211 X A M P L E . c o m` (0xC3 0x89 preserved, É NOT folded to é) in both line2 and line7 `http://ÉXAMPLE.com:80/`; (\"http\",\"a\\x7f\\xffb\",...) -> od `61 177 377 62` in line2 and again in line7; \"日本.example\" -> line7 `http://日本.example:80/`; \"ÄÖÜ.example\" -> line2 `ÄÖÜ.example`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO length limit, truncation, or validation by length. Host is stored and re-emitted in full at every size tested; line7 length == 7 + len(host) + 4 for \"http://\"+host+\":80/\".",
    "evidence": "lengths 63,64,253,254,255,256,400,1000,2000 all: exit=0, line2 length == input length and string-compares equal to input (\"verbatim\"), valid=1, empty stderr. e.g. in_len=1000 -> line2_len=1000 verbatim, line7_len=1011; 400-char host -> line7 = `http://aaa...aaa:80/` length 411; a 170-char mixed `A.%41A.%41...` host is also verbatim"
  },
  {
    "rule": "host has NO effect on validity as a character class — no host value observed makes is_valid 0. Instead is_valid (line 8) == (host is non-empty) OR (path is non-empty); host merely contributes its non-emptiness, and even a single-space host counts as non-empty.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"x\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 empty but line8 `1`; (\"\",\" \",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 empty, line8 `1`; (\"\",\"EXAMPLE.COM\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 empty, line8 `1`; (\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 `http://`, line8 `0`; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 `/`, line8 `1`; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"q\",\"\") -> line7 `?q`, line8 `0`; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"f\") -> line7 `#f`, line8 `0`; (\"\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line8 `0`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "to_string reflects host by plain concatenation, gated entirely on scheme: if scheme != \"\" then out += scheme + \"://\" + host + (port >= 0 ? \":\" + to_string(port) : \"\"). The \"//\" is emitted even when host is empty.",
    "evidence": "(\"http\",\"\",\"-1\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 `http:///`; (\"http\",\"\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> `http://:80/`; (\"http\",\"x\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> `http://x:80` (no trailing slash added); (\"http\",\"localhost\",\"-1\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> `http://localhost/`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "If scheme is empty, the entire authority — including a non-empty host and the port — is DROPPED from to_string, even though host() still reports it verbatim.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"EXAMPLE.COM\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line2 `EXAMPLE.COM`, line3 `80`, line7 `/`; (\"\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 empty; (\"\",\"[::1]\",\"-1\",\"/x\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 `/x`; (\"\",\"//example.com\",\"80\",\"/x\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 `/x`"
  },
  {
    "rule": "No diagnostics ever: every host value tested returns exit code 0 with zero bytes on stderr and exactly 8 printed values (newline-embedded hosts still print 8 values, just spread over more physical lines).",
    "evidence": "All runs printed `exit=0`; `test15_executable http EXAMPLE.COM 80 / '' '' 2>&1 >/dev/null | wc -c` -> 0; stdout line count -> 8; long-host loop reported stderr=0 bytes for every length including 2000"
  }
]

STATED UNCERTAINTIES (resolve these definitively by experiment):
[
  "A NUL byte (0x00) cannot be passed through argv, so I could not test whether a host containing an embedded NUL is truncated. All other byte values 0x01-0xFF were tested as verbatim.",
  "I could not distinguish 'host stored verbatim' from 'host stored verbatim after a no-op transformation pass' — the observable behavior is identical. Only the externally visible result (host() and to_string()) is proven verbatim.",
  "Because scheme IS lowercased while host is not, this looks like an intentional asymmetry (or a bug) in from_components, but the binary gives no way to confirm intent.",
  "Hosts above 2000 chars were not tested (2000 worked fine with valid=1 and no truncation); there may be some very large limit driven by allocation, not by URL logic.",
  "is_valid == (!host.empty() || !path.empty()) is consistent with all 10 emptiness-probe cases I ran, but query/fragment/port were only varied in a few combinations, so a more exotic interaction is not fully excluded (this is primarily the validity dimension, not host).",
  "Whether host affects to_string when the scheme is empty but a port is set was tested only with port 80 and -1 (both dropped the authority entirely); other port values were not swept in the empty-scheme case."
]

Then write finalSpec: complete, unambiguous pseudocode for this dimension that a Rust implementer can follow with zero guesswork. Include the exact literal outputs for any case where the behavior is surprising.
